========================================================================
                         tsed
                    ed in TypeScript
========================================================================

Copyright (c) 2026 wordgitet
SPDX-License-Identifier: 0BSD

tsed is a real implementation of the classic ed line editor in TypeScript,
running on Bun.  That sentence is also the joke.

PROJECT SNAPSHOT

    target       POSIX platforms
    runtime      Bun
    status       research toy
    license      0BSD

This is a research toy, not a claim of complete POSIX conformance.  It targets
POSIX platforms only.  Windows is not supported.

Regular expressions use a small native Node-API binding to the host POSIX libc.
The active LC_CTYPE and LC_COLLATE locales therefore define character classes,
ranges, equivalence classes, and collating elements.  Bun and a compatible
native build are required when a matching npm prebuild is unavailable.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
INSTALLING FROM NPM
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The alpha package includes the TypeScript implementation and a small Bun
launcher.  Bun must already be installed and available in PATH:

    npm install --global @wordgitet/tsed@alpha

This installs both command names:

    tsed
    ed

------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUILDING AND RUNNING
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Install Bun and build the editor from the repository root:

    bun install
    bun run build

On a platform without a bundled native prebuild, build the backend explicitly:

    bun run build:native

The build creates two command names:

    ./dist/ed
    ./dist/tsed

Both accept the following arguments:

    ./dist/ed [-s] [-p prompt] [--] [pathname]

The minimum usable Bun version is not known.  Development currently uses Bun
1.4.0, so trying an older version is at your own risk.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
A TINY SESSION
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Run the editor:

    $ ./dist/ed -s

Then enter:

    a
    hello, world
    .
    s/world/POSIX/
    p
    Q

The editor prints:

    hello, POSIX

The a command appends input until a line containing only a dot.  The s command
substitutes text, p prints the current line, and Q quits without a
modified-buffer warning.

